  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA thunderstorm cluster is expected to develop near northern IL\ninto Saturday morning that will eventually travel ESE through \nthe lower Great Lakes in conjunction with a mid-level shortwave.\n\nAny severe risks associated with damaging wind reside in an \nearlier arrival (Sat aftn/eve) that sees the MCS driving SE \nthrough the far eastern OH zones (Muskingum/Coshocton Counties) \nalong the CAPE gradient. This is dependent on anything that\ndevelops ahead of the MCS as the severe threat shows less\nconfidence in occurring. Recent runs of the CAMs suggest the \nthreat being pushed more into the overnight. This focuses more \non the flooding threat. Looking at 1 HR FFG guidance across the \nmost likely area shows 1.2 to 1.4 FFG. Its worth mentioning that\nthe NBM mean paints some 1.5 to 1.7 precip amounts along I-70 \nand south. let along the area in the higher quartile will easily\nsurpass the 1 HR FFG. At this time, may be leaning towards a \nFlood Watch. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Stable air develops in the wake of the morning\nMCS Sunday that could generally last most of the day and limit \nany activity in the afternoon/evening to spotty, sub-severe \ncoverage.\n\nHowever, there remains a low probability that enough clearing\noccurs in the wake for afternoon destabilization resulting in\nSBCAPE values rising to 1000-1800 J/kg in a 25-30kt sheared\nenvironment. If there is enough lift in the lingering NW flow, a\nfew instances of severe storms with damaging wind can't be ruled\nout along with non-zero flood risks where rain previously fell.\nThe CAMs are showing some development Sunday afternoon and\nevening but it can be argued that the strength of the convection\non Sunday evening is dependent on early morning convection. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nScattered showers and low probability thunderstorms may linger\nwithin the increasingly stabilizing environment Sunday night\ninto Monday. However, high pressure building in from the west\nunder drier NW flow favors a drying trend in storm activity by\nMonday evening that lasts generally til Wednesday.\n\nThough timing can change, the next system featuring\nshower/thunderstorm chances favors the mid to latter week period\nnext week.",
